---
import LessonLayout from '../../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
const lessonRef = "CCC 1776-1802";
const lessonScripture = "Rom 2:14-16; 1 Jn 3:19-21; Mt 6:22-23; Ps 139:23-24; 1 Tim 1:5";
---
<LessonLayout title="Conscience &mdash; Workbook" cic={lessonRef} scripture={lessonScripture}>

<section class="content">

<h2>Reviewing the Essentials</h2>

<ol>
<li>What is the Catechism's definition of conscience? (CCC 1776)</li>
<li>What are the five essential means for forming conscience?</li>
<li>What is the difference between a "lax" conscience and a "scrupulous" conscience?</li>
<li>What is the difference between "invincible" and "vincible" ignorance?</li>
<li>Is conscience autonomous? What does that mean?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>For Reflection and Writing</h2>

<div class="question-block">
<p><strong>1.</strong> Describe a time when you knew, deep down, that something was wrong — even if everyone around you was doing it. Where did that knowledge come from? How did you feel afterward?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p><strong>2.</strong> When was the last time you deliberately ignored your conscience? What happened? What did you learn?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p><strong>3.</strong> Do you tend toward a lax conscience ("everything is fine") or a scrupulous one ("everything is a sin")? What might be causing this tendency?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p><strong>4.</strong> Imagine you're talking to a friend who says: "My conscience tells me this is okay, so it must be okay." How would you respond using what you learned about conscience not being autonomous?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p><strong>5.</strong> St. Thomas More said: "I die the king's good servant, but God's first." Is there a situation in your life where you're tempted to reverse that order?</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Practical Activities</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>The Ignatian Examen:</strong> Look up "The Daily Examen" by St. Ignatius and practice it for one week. Write about what you noticed.</li>
<li><strong>Conscience formation:</strong> Read CCC 1776-1802 slowly. Underline anything that surprises you or challenges you.</li>
<li><strong>The Psalm 139 prayer:</strong> Pray "Search me, O God, and know my heart" every morning this week. What did God reveal to you?</li>
<li><strong>Confession:</strong> If you've been ignoring your conscience about something, make a plan to go to confession this week.</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Memory Verse</h2>

<blockquote>
<p>"Search me, O God, and know my heart; test me and know my anxious thoughts. See if there is any offensive way in me, and lead me in the way everlasting."</p>
<cite>Psalm 139:23-24</cite>
</blockquote>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Prayer for This Week</h2>

<p>
Lord Jesus, you know my heart better than I know myself. Search me and reveal where my conscience has been numbed, where I've ignored your voice, where I've rationalized my sins. Give me the courage to face what you reveal. Form my conscience by your Word, your Church, and your Spirit. Make me quick to recognize evil and quick to choose good. Give me the strength of St. Thomas More and St. John Henry Newman — to follow conscience even when it costs me.</p>

<p><em>Amen.</em></p>

</section>

